t with the prayer  for Relief Neiman seeks 100 million  dollars in Damages and while that's  certainly a jaw-dropping number it has  zero basis in reality he certainly  pulled that one out of his ass whenever  someone files a lawsuit they can put any  number they want and in a complaint like  this that serves potentially to recover  damages but also serves to try and  rehabilitate someone's reputation uh  it's there to grab headlines which it  certainly did but in a case like this  any prayer for Relief whether it's one  dollar or 100 million dollars means  absolutely nothing but let's move on to  the rest of this lawsuit and start with  jurisdiction because Neiman has to show  why federal courts in St Louis even have  jurisdiction to decide this matter and  the fact that Neiman filed suit in  Missouri raises all kinds of questions  to hear a case a court has to have both  personal jurisdiction and subject matter  jurisdiction now subject matter  jurisdiction is a requirement that a  court has the power to hear a specific  kind of claim and for the moment it  seems that the courts having Federal  jurisdiction might make sense because  the complaint at the moment asserts a  claim for a violation of the Sherman  Antitrust Act which is a federal law and  uh there might be something called  diversity jurisdiction given the  residency of all of the defendants but  there's also the personal jurisdiction  requirement that the court has power  over the defendants based on usually  what's called minimum contacts with that  forum and the most common way to get  personal jurisdiction over a person or a  business is to sue them where they live  or do business or where the tort  occurred but here it's entirely possible  that some or all the defendants could  get the suit dismissed at least without  prejudice meaning that the suit could be  refiled given their lack of connection  to St Louis now Neiman lives in  Connecticut Nakamura lives in Florida  Carlson and his company are from Norway  and chess.com is a Nevada LLC while  wrench lives in Utah the only one with  any links to Missouri is Carlson who  allegedly made the defamatory remarks  that first arose out of his defeat in  the sink field Cup in St Louis so why  would Neiman Sue in Missouri instead of  his home in Connecticut well some have  speculated it's because of Connecticut's  strong anti-slap statute which dismisses  lawsuits that are brought simply to  retaliate against defendants exercising  of free speech which would have required  Neiman to show evidence for every single  element of his claims against every  single defendant to avoid dismissal and  in contrast Missouri's anti-slap law is  basically non-existent and it only  applies to comments and public hearings  which certainly wouldn't stop Neiman's  suit so let's talk about Neiman's  defamation claims if you haven't seen  are dozens of videos tackling the topic  of defamation definition is a tort that  creates civil liability for false  statements that injure a third party's  reputation Neiman has sued the  defendants for slander which is verbal  defamation and libel written defamation  but there are several barriers that  could make these claims hard to win even  if the cheating allegations are proven  to be untrue for starters definition is  only actionable if a defendant makes a  false statement of verifiable fact  meaning the assertion must be capable of  definitively being proven true or false  and opinions are by Nature not  defamatory if they nearly Express the  speaker's commentary based on Public  Information and here Carlson accusing  Neiman of cheating after losing at the  sinkfield Cub is probably not an  assertion of fact but a opinion based on  disclosed fact and as some have said it  may be a stupid opinion it may be a  wrong opinion but it's an opinion a  conclusion reached based on the observed  play not a statement of fact it can't be  defamatory and to Prevail Neiman would  have to prove that Carlson didn't  actually think Neiman cheated when he  made those claims a very high if not  impossible bar to clear similarly  Nakamura may be shielded from the  affirmation claims to the extent that  he's only giving his opinion and  commentary on Neiman's alleged cheating  based on public facts and not to fear  the same goes for a legal commentary  YouTube channel giving legal commentary  on a chess cheating scandal but the  complaint does suggest that chess.com's  conclusion that Neiman cheated is based  on on undisclosed facts and sometimes  implying undisclosed facts or explicitly  stating that there are undisclosed facts  can lead to defamation and the chess.com  report alleges that Neiman privately  confessed to additional cheating  offenses in a private August 2020 phone  call with chess.com's Danny wrench which  the complaint denies however the  chess.com report does include private  Communications between Neiman and wrench  between August 2020 and January 2021  that shows the zoom call took place  Neiman acknowledging being banned for  cheating and calling said consequences  completely fair and that's certainly  going to hurt Neiman's credibility  unless he can show that those private  messages were faked or taken out of  context another potential barrier to  recovery Neiman's Fame and notoriety  means that he could be classified as a  public figure subject to the heightened  actual malice standard to prove actual  Mouse which again has nothing to do with  hating someone one must prove that the  statements were made with knowledge of  being false or Reckless disregard for  their truth or falsity the actual malice  standard also applies to limited purpose  public figures which refers to to one  who voluntarily participates in a  discussion about a particular  controversy and has access to the media  to get his or her views across this is  opposed to the normal standard which is  just negligence with respect to false  statements but Neiman's almost trumpian  Praise of how famous and successful he  is that's contained in the complaint  only reinforces the idea that he should  be treated as a public figure subject to  the actual malice standard but that's a  double-edged sword because this  complaint is potentially to redress  grievances in court and get damages but  is also supposed to try to rehabilitate  Neiman's own reputation and get eyeballs  on it so it goes both ways but given  this high standard of proof Neiman might  not have any luck against chess.com in  court and as Bloomberg columnist Stephen  L Carter points out lawsuits by  individuals accused of cheating have  mostly been unsuccessful with courts  often unwilling to second guess a gaming  organization's own findings of  wrongdoing as an example Carter cites to  a 2010 Indiana case where a golf  teaching pro unsuccessfully sued a  colleague for calling him a cheat for  claiming a different classification than  the one to which he was entitled in that  case the professional Golf Association  agreed with the colleague's allegations  following an investigation and the court  dismissed the defamation claim because  the statements were true as Carter notes  the court didn't do its own  investigation rather the organization's  own judgment was sufficient to Shield  the defendant from liability and doubly  so it's hard to win an Anti-Trust and  intentional interference claim in the  absence of prevailing on a defamation  claim and those remaining causes of  action are also not particularly strong  based on the allegations made in the  complaint for antitrust claims in this  context it's likely that Neiman would  have to plead enough facts to convince a  jury that if true the defendants entered  into an actual agreement to Blacklist  Neiman but Neiman and chess.com are not  competitors and complaining the  chess.com and Carlson won't voluntarily  play with him doesn't necessarily  establish an illegal agreement and  simply alleging that the defendants did  similar thing things at the same time  isn't enough to allege an agreement in  Restraint of trade and additionally  tortious interference allows for a claim  for damages when a defendant wrongfully  interferes with an existing contractual  or business relationship the key there  being wrongful interference with an  existing contractual relationship the  complaint makes conclusory allegations  that defendants intentionally interfered  with Neiman's relationship with a series  of Chess related entities to coerce them  to terminate their relationships with  him and Blacklist him for future trade  but these are tough claims to make  because the complaint doesn't actually  allege any conduct directed at these  entities and to Prevail on a civil  conspiracy cause of action a plaintiff  has to prove that two or more defendants  again entered into an agreement to  commit a wrong and did actually commit a  tortious act in furtherance of that  agreement that caused injury to a third  party and Neiman makes conclusory  allegations that the defendants agreed  to do bad stuff to hurt Naaman but  doesn't actually provide the factual  basis suggesting an agreement to do that  and quoting Texas Judge John chupp three  people all liking the same cat picture  on the internet is not a conspiracy but  the thing about real conspiracies is  that you rarely have evidence of that  agreement at face value so if this  lawsuit does make it past the motion to  dismiss stage into Discovery I'm sure  the very first thing that Neiman will  ask for are all Communications related  to agreements between all of these  parties and who knows maybe those  agreements exist but to understand  whether Neiman cheated or whether  Carlson is being a sore loser it would  really help to understand statistics and  probability and today's sponsor  brilliant is exactly the place to help  brilliant is an online learning platform  for stem that replaces lecture videos  with Hands-On interactive lessons  brilliant is for Curious 